            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

AIR SEALING



During the blower door test, we identified that overall your house is built fairly air tight given the age, but it does have some minor areas of excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** Around the attic hatch. This can be sealed with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack".



** The bathroom exhaust fans in both bathrooms. The exhaust fans likely have dampers that aren't sealing well and could be replaced with new ENERGY STAR* exhaust fans with butterfly dampers.



** The old, vented dryer penetration in the wall. As you have a new, ventless dryer, this penetration could be sealed up with a spray foam application.



** In the header space of the crawlspace. During the on-stie investigation we noticed that the spray foam application in the header spaces was old, had shrunk, and been poorly sealed with a layer of poly. This is a significant area of air leakage into the main floor of your home, bringing cool air from the crawlspace with it. By re-applying spray foam around the existing application you would adequately seal up this area of air leakage. A contractor could also be hired to complete this work.



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss, and increased comfort levels. This is estimated to save $40 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 1.7 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.











</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

MAIN WALLS



The main walls of your home appear to be 2x4 construction with batt insulation, and 2 inches of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation on the outside (R22). I have modelled adding 2 additional inches (R10) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation to the exterior (total R32). Due to the large amount of labour involved in adding insulation to the exterior of your home, and the high cost, this recommendation should only be taken in conjunction with a siding upgrade.



Savings: By adding 2 inches (R10) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls, you could potentially save $ 170 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.3 GJ, and qualify for up to a $ 1500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



</mainWalls>
                <foundation>CRAWLSPACE - WALLS &amp; FLOOR



Your crawlspace walls appear to be 2x4 framing with batt insulation in the cavities and 1.5 inches of rigid polystyrene insulation on the outside. This amount of insulation is not uniform throughout, so I have modelled adding insulation to the entire crawlsoace inculding the exposed, below grade concrete foundation to bring the entire wall to R20. I've also modelled adding R10 insulation to surface of the ground, as it too is taking heat away from your heated crawl space.



Savings: By adding the above level of insulation to the crawlspace, you could potentially save $310 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 12 GJ, and qualify for up to a $500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program









</foundation>
                <windows>

WINDOWS



All of the windows in the main floor of this house (8 total) are older double pane, vinyl windows, that are in good condition. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $130 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 5.1 GJ. 



Replacing windows is a high cost upgrade, with long payback times based on savings. In your case a window upgrade should only be completed if a siding upgrade is planned, and should be completed in combination with an insulation upgrade.



There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.





</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

HOT WATER SYSTEM - Propane Instantaneous Upgrade from Electric



You currently have a 184 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. You may want to consider replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $1,290 per year in energy costs, slightly increase your annual energy usage by 0.1 GJ,  and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program





</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

HEATING SYSTEM



Your house is currently heated with an older 84% efficient oil furnace. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified, high-efficiency, condensing, propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a 95% AFUE condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $550 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 22 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



Note: If you do plan to upgrade your wall insulation (see upgrade #4) this should be done before upgrading your furnace. This is because the heating requirements of your home will change if insulation were added.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



SUPPLEMENTARY HEATING - WOOD STOVE



I have modeled the estimated energy savings (net of oil saved and the cost of wood in Yellowknife at the $420/cord "purchased" rate) to provide details on your current savings from using your wood stove for about 50% of the heating requirement of your home. Getting that wood heat into the far bedrooms is something your heating contractor can work on by using the furnace fan to circulate the wood heat throughout the house.



Rebates &amp; Savings: By using your 77% efficient Blaze King Sorocco wood stove for 50% of the heating requirements of the main living area of your house, and purchasing wood from a local supplier it is estimated that your cost savings for heating are negligible. If you were to harvest your own wood I estimate that you would save $670 per year in heating costs.









</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>ADDITIONAL COMMENTS



LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



In your home, the water circulation pump was observed to be a non-ECM pump, so this is a simple upgrade that can be completed immediately, and will provide instant electricity savings.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

VENTILATION



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house: 

        - two bathroom fans



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove moisture without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

